<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kanhadadeva</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1311">1311</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kanhadadeva was the Chahamana (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan</a>) king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> at the turn of the 14th century. He resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, whose armies took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siwana">Siwana</a> in 1308 and then besieged Jalor, capturing it in 1311; Kanhadadeva and his son Viramadeva were killed. His stand is celebrated in the 15th-century epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanhadade-prabandha">Kanhadade Prabandha</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kanhadadeva was the son of Samantasimha, whom he served as heir apparent, sharing the administration of Javalipura, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalore</a>, from at least 1296, as a pillar inscription of that year at Topkhana and the Chohtan inscription of 1299 record. He was also known as Dasam Saligrama and Gokulanatha. No inscription of his own reign survives; the last of his father's is dated 1305, so he appears to have become sole ruler about then.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1299 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>'s generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-khan">Ulugh Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nusrat-khan">Nusrat Khan</a> marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanhadade-prabandha">Kanhadade Prabandha</a> says the prince refused them passage, and the 17th-century Nainsi ri Khyat says that on their return his forces attacked their camp near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalore</a> while the Muslim converts Muhammad Shah and his brothers mutinied. Historians are divided: Dasharatha Sharma accepts Nainsi's account, while Kishori Saran Lal doubts it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> chronicles mention no part played by Jalore. Later texts also credit him with rescuing the Somnath idol, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau">Amir Khusrau</a>, Barani and Jinaprabha say it was taken to Delhi, and the story may be a later invention.</p><p>After taking Ranthambore in 1301 and Chittor in 1303, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalore</a>'s neighbour. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanhadade-prabandha">Kanhadade Prabandha</a> tells of a princess Piroja in love with Kanhadadeva's son; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Firishta</a>, writing in the 16th century, says Kanhadadeva visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> but rejected the terms offered; Nainsi tells of a refused marriage for his son Viramadeva. None of these accounts is reliable. By 1310 Alauddin had subdued every kingdom around Jalore, and he appears to have attacked simply to end its independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The first armies sent against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalore</a> failed to take the fort, and in 1311 a stronger force under Malik Kamaluddin Gurg defeated and killed Kanhadadeva. Padmanabha, a court poet of the later rulers of Jalore, praised him in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanhadade-prabandha">Kanhadade Prabandha</a> of 1455, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> with Old Rajasthani and some Gujarati influences, which presents him as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> king and a saviour against the Muslims who had desecrated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somnath-temple">Somnath temple</a>, and demonises Muslims.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kanhadadeva” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Kanhadadeva</b> resisted Alauddin Khalji, who took Siwana and then besieged Jalor, capturing it in 1311; the king and his son Viramadeva were killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-jalor">Chahamanas of Jalor</a></li></ul></section>
